<!--
  DreamTeam lint config (DRE-193 / M10-E polish sweep).

  The remaining lint warnings on this module are all "a newer version is
  available" advisories (kotlin / AGP / Compose BOM / compileSdk / targetSdk /
  ktor / sqlite / junit / coroutines / kotest / serialization) plus the
  ObsoleteSdkInt note on the adaptive launcher icon folder. Acting on the
  version ones means bumping a dependency or the SDK — explicitly OUT OF SCOPE
  here: DRE-184 Milestone 10 is Tier-1-only, "no new spend, no new dependency"
  (gradle/libs.versions.toml + build.gradle.kts versions are deliberately
  pinned). Those bumps are a separate founder-gated decision, not a polish fix.

  ObsoleteSdkInt fires on `mipmap-anydpi-v26` (lint suggests merging into
  `mipmap-anydpi`), but AAPT2 fails to resolve the launcher icon from a bare
  `mipmap-anydpi` source set in this AGP setup (verified: resource not found,
  even after :app:clean). The `-v26` qualifier is the working configuration, so
  the advisory is suppressed rather than acted on — minSdk is already 26, so the
  guard is redundant-but-harmless, not a correctness issue.

  These five detectors are set to `ignore` — a clean, documented suppression
  (DRE-193 acceptance: "clean suppressions/baseline allowed"). When the founder
  green-lights a dependency/SDK refresh (or AGP accepts the anydpi merge), remove
  these entries and let lint report again. Every other lint check stays at its
  default severity and must pass clean.
-->
<lint>
    <issue id="NewerVersionAvailable" severity="ignore" />
    <issue id="AndroidGradlePluginVersion" severity="ignore" />
    <issue id="GradleDependency" severity="ignore" />
    <issue id="OldTargetApi" severity="ignore" />
    <issue id="ObsoleteSdkInt" severity="ignore" />
</lint>
